= Situated cognition — Simple =

'''Situated cognition''' is the idea that thinking and learning depend partly on the situation in which they happen.

The situation can include:

* the physical environment;
* the body;
* tools;
* other people;
* language;
* cultural practices;
* the task being performed.

This does not mean people are unable to think abstractly.

It means that context can change what is easy to notice, remember, understand, or do.

== Learning in real situations ==

A student may know how to solve an arithmetic problem on paper but struggle to apply the same mathematics in a workshop.

Another person may solve the workshop problem easily using tools but struggle with a formal equation.

Situated cognition asks what the activity and context contribute.

John Seely Brown, Allan Collins, and Paul Duguid argued that knowledge is partly a product of the activity and culture in which it is learned and used.<ref name="brown">[https://doi.org/10.3102/0013189X018001032 Brown, Collins and Duguid (1989), “Situated Cognition and the Culture of Learning.”]</ref>

== Context is not just background ==

In a weak sense, everyone agrees that context influences behavior.

Stronger situated theories argue that the environment, tools, or social activity can become part of the cognitive process itself.

These stronger claims are more debated.

Situated cognition is therefore a family of theories rather than one single doctrine.<ref name="roth">[https://doi.org/10.1002/wcs.1242 Roth and Jornet (2013), “Situated cognition.”]</ref>

== Knowing and doing ==

Traditional education sometimes separates knowledge from use.

Situated learning emphasizes learning through meaningful activity.

A mechanic learns not only facts about engines but how to diagnose a real machine.

A scientist learns not only formulas but how to collect evidence, use instruments, and interpret uncertain results.

== Cognitive apprenticeship ==

'''Cognitive apprenticeship''' adapts apprenticeship-style learning to thinking skills.

A teacher or expert can:

* demonstrate;
* explain reasoning;
* coach;
* provide temporary support;
* gradually reduce help;
* encourage independent problem solving.

The idea is to make expert thinking visible inside meaningful activity.

== Communities of practice ==

People often learn by participating in groups that share a practice.

Examples include:

* laboratories;
* software teams;
* medical professions;
* crafts;
* sports.

Newcomers can begin with simpler tasks and gradually participate more fully.

This is sometimes called '''legitimate peripheral participation'''.

== Transfer ==

'''Transfer''' means using knowledge in a new situation.

Situated cognition predicts that transfer can be difficult when contexts differ.

But transfer is possible.

It improves when people understand the deeper principle, practice across several examples, and explicitly compare situations.

So situated cognition does not mean knowledge is trapped permanently in one context.

== Tools can change the task ==

A person using paper, calculator, map, or software may solve a problem differently from someone working only from memory.

The tool can store information or make relationships visible.

This changes the cognitive work required.

== Embodied cognition ==

[[Embodied cognition]] emphasizes how bodily structure and sensorimotor activity contribute to cognition.

It overlaps with situated cognition.

But situated cognition also emphasizes social, cultural, and environmental context.

== Distributed cognition ==

[[Distributed cognition]] studies cognitive processes spread across people and artifacts.

For example, a navigation team may coordinate using instruments, charts, and specialized roles.

Situated cognition is broader; distributed cognition makes the system boundary more explicit.

== Affordances ==

An affordance is an opportunity for action relative to an agent.

A door handle affords pulling.

A chair affords sitting.

The environment therefore does not simply provide information; it can structure possible actions.

== Robotics ==

Robots can exploit environmental structure instead of building complete internal models.

A simple robot may react directly to sensors and still navigate successfully.

This demonstrates an engineering strategy.

It does not prove that human cognition works in exactly the same way.

== What evidence would support a situated claim? ==

Researchers can compare the same underlying task across contexts.

They can vary:

* access to tools;
* social support;
* physical setting;
* task framing.

Then they can measure performance and transfer.

A context effect supports the claim that context matters.

It does not prove every theory of situated cognition.

